course: biology
lesson: 264
title: How scientists find out: naturalists, Darwin, the microscope and wildlife filming
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Science about living things starts with looking very carefully: a girl in the park who draws a beetle in her notebook, writes the date and counts its legs is doing what naturalists have always done.
explain: Careful notes let other people check and compare; Charles Darwin filled notebooks on his round-the-world voyage on the ship Beagle, then worked for over 20 years before publishing his big idea in 1859.
explain: New tools open hidden worlds: the microscope showed cells and bacteria, ringing birds' legs shows where they travel, and camera traps film snow leopards that people almost never see.
explain: A good experiment is a fair test that changes only one thing at a time, and good scientists repeat it, try to prove their own ideas wrong, and are not ashamed to say «I was wrong».
explain: Cartoons show animals talking, wearing clothes and acting out stories, and even some old nature films set up scenes, like lemmings herded off a cliff in 1958; honest filmmakers today tell you how a scene was filmed.
fact: Naturalists watch living things carefully and write down what they see, so that others can check and compare; Gilbert White kept notes on the birds of his village, Selborne, in the 1700s, and his book is still read.
fact: Charles Darwin sailed around the world on the Beagle from 1831 to 1836, collecting animals, plants and fossils.
fact: In the Galapagos Islands, Darwin noticed that animals on different islands were slightly different, and he published his famous book in 1859, after more than 20 years of work, because his idea was very surprising and he wanted plenty of careful evidence first.
fact: Darwin's finches have beaks suited to seeds, insects or cactus; over many generations birds whose beaks fitted the food survived better, so beaks changed.
fact: In the 1970s Peter and Rosemary Grant measured finch beaks changing in just a few years after a drought.
fact: Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a cloth seller rather than a trained scientist, made tiny single-lens microscopes that magnified about 200 times or more and showed living cells and bacteria.
fact: Wildlife filmmakers wait quietly in hides for weeks or months and use hidden cameras, drones and night cameras so as not to disturb animals; a single minute of film can take weeks.
fact: Camera traps take pictures when an animal moves past, which helps count rare animals such as snow leopards, and each snow leopard can be recognised by its own spot pattern.
fact: Bird ringing tells scientists where birds travel and how long they live; an albatross called Wisdom, ringed in 1956, was still laying eggs at over 70 years old.
fact: A fair test changes only one thing at a time and keeps everything else the same, and scientists repeat experiments to check that a result is not luck.
fact: Some old nature films staged scenes, like a 1958 film that showed lemmings being pushed off a cliff, though real lemmings do not leap to their deaths; today good films tell you when a scene was filmed in a studio, a zoo or a special set.
fact: From 1960 Jane Goodall watched chimpanzees in Gombe, Tanzania, and saw one poke a grass stem into a termite mound to fish out termites, showing that chimps make and use tools; before that, many thought only humans made tools.
fact: Science changes its mind when new evidence comes, as with the old idea of fierce «alpha» wolves fighting to lead a pack, which scientists later said was wrong for wild wolves.
fact: Ordinary people help science by counting birds, insects or plants, which is called citizen science; the RSPB's Big Garden Birdwatch has hundreds of thousands of people taking part each year, so scientists can see how bird numbers change over the years.
exercise: What do naturalists do? | Watch living things carefully and write down what they see | Keep lots of wild animals as pets at home and in the garden shed | Only study animals in books | Invent new kinds of animals
exercise: Why are careful notes important in science? | Others can check and compare them | They make the scientist rich and famous | They are needed to get into a zoo for free | They stop the animals from moving about
exercise: What was the name of the ship Darwin sailed around the world on? | The Beagle | The Titanic | The Mayflower | The Victory
exercise: When was Darwin's voyage? | From 1831 to 1836 | From 1492 to 1493 | From 1914 to 1918 | From 1969 to 1972
exercise: What did Darwin notice in the Galapagos Islands? | Animals on different islands were slightly different | All of the animals on every island were exactly the same | There were no animals there at all | The animals could talk to each other
exercise: How long did Darwin work before publishing his famous book in 1859? | More than 20 years | One week | Two months | Exactly one year
exercise: Why do Darwin's finches have different beaks? | Over many generations, beaks that fitted the food helped birds survive | Each bird chose its own beak shape when it hatched and kept it all its life | The finches broke their beaks on rocks | Darwin trimmed them with scissors
exercise: What did Peter and Rosemary Grant find after a drought? | Finch beaks changed in just a few years | Finches turned into completely different birds, like owls | All the finches flew away for ever and never came back | Beaks never change at all, whatever happens
exercise: What was Antonie van Leeuwenhoek's job? | Cloth seller | Ship's captain | King's doctor | School teacher
exercise: Why do wildlife filmmakers wait quietly in hides? | So as not to disturb the animals | Because animals cannot see cameras | To keep themselves warm in the cold | Because filming is not allowed outside
exercise: How do camera traps work? | They take pictures when an animal moves past | They catch the animals in a cage so they can be looked at | They follow animals with a person | They film only at midday
exercise: How can scientists tell one snow leopard from another? | Each has its own spot pattern | By the length of its tail only | They all wear numbered collars | By the colour of its eyes alone
exercise: What can a ring on a bird's leg tell scientists? | Where the bird travels and how long it lives | What the bird had for its breakfast that morning | How loudly the bird sings | What the bird is thinking
exercise: What makes a fair test? | Changing only one thing and keeping everything else the same | Changing everything at once, so that you save lots of time and work | Doing it once and never repeating it | Choosing the answer before you start
exercise: What did a 1958 nature film do with lemmings? | Set up a scene where they were pushed off a cliff | Filmed them flying in great flocks over the sea, like birds | Showed them building dams | Filmed them on the Moon
exercise: What did Jane Goodall see a chimpanzee do? | Poke a grass stem into a termite mound to fish for termites | Build a little wooden house up in a tree to sleep in | Read a book with pictures | Light a fire to cook food
exercise: What is citizen science? | Ordinary people helping science by counting and recording things | Scientists who live and work in the big cities | Science done only by governments | A school subject about towns
test: In a cartoon, animals talk and wear clothes, like Tom and Jerry or a talking lion. How do real scientists find out how animals behave? | By watching and recording quietly, often for a very long time | By asking the animals questions | By reading cartoon scripts | By dressing up as animals
test: «a girl in my class draws bugs in a notebook, so weird» What is the best answer? | She is doing just what naturalists do, and her notes could be useful | Drawing animals is useless for science, so she is wasting her time | Only grown-ups can do science | Notes must be typed to count
test: Count them: a girl draws 3 beetles and 2 spiders in her notebook. How many legs does she draw if she gets them right? | 34 | 30 | 25 | 40
test: Count them: in the garden birdwatch Sam counts 5 robins, then says «no wait, 6», and 3 blackbirds. How many birds does he report? | 9 | 8 | 14 | 6
test: Why might Darwin have waited so long before publishing his idea? | He wanted lots of careful evidence for a very surprising idea | He forgot where he had put his notes | His ship was lost at sea for 20 years with all of his notebooks on board | He was waiting for a computer
test: You want to test if plants grow better with music. Which plan is a fair test? | Two same plants with the same light and water, only one hears music | One plant in the sun with music, and one in a dark cupboard with no music at all | Many plants given different water and music | One plant with music, watched for one hour
test: «a film showed lemmings jumping off cliffs so they do it, it's on tv» What is right? | That 1958 scene was set up by the filmmakers; lemmings do not leap to their deaths | Yes, they do it every year | Yes, because lemmings cannot see cliffs and simply run straight off the edge in a crowd | No, lemmings live only in zoos
test: «the scientist said she was wrong, so she's a rubbish scientist» What is right? | Changing your mind with new evidence is how science gets better | Good scientists are never wrong, so they never change their minds | Being wrong means science is useless | Scientists should hide their mistakes
test: A bird ringed in England is found a year later in Africa. What does that show? | The bird flew thousands of kilometres on migration | The ring fell off in Britain and blew there in the wind | Someone caught it and carried it there by plane in a box | Birds are born in Africa and fly to England only once
test: Why was Jane Goodall's discovery so surprising? | Many thought only humans made and used tools | No one knew chimpanzees ate food | Chimpanzees had never been seen before | Tools were only invented in 1960
test: What would happen if thousands of children counted garden birds every year? | Scientists could see how bird numbers change over time | The birds would leave the gardens | The counts would be useless because children made them | Every count would be exactly the same
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of a fair test as conducted in a scientific investigation. | A single factor is varied whilst all other conditions are maintained constant | All factors are varied simultaneously for the sake of economy and speed of work | The result is decided before the commencement of the test | The test is conducted once only and never repeated
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the voyage of Charles Darwin. | He circumnavigated the globe aboard the Beagle between 1831 and 1836, collecting animals, plants and fossils | He sailed aboard the Mayflower in the year 1620 | He published his principal work immediately upon his return in 1836 | He conducted his studies exclusively within England
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of the observations of Jane Goodall at Gombe? | It was established that chimpanzees fashion and employ tools, a capacity formerly attributed solely to humans | It was established that chimpanzees are incapable of learning | It was established that chimpanzees subsist exclusively upon fruit | It was established that chimpanzees construct dwellings of timber
exam: Ascertain the purpose served by the attachment of rings to the legs of birds. | The determination of the routes of their travel and the length of their lives | The improvement of their flight | The protection of their legs from predators | The identification of their song
